  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...There is a marginal risk of excessive rainfall \nfor southern Virginia and into the Carolinas today. \n\nA surface front bisects the forecast area, basically aligned\nwith Highway 58 near the VA/NC border. This front will be the\nfocus for scattered to numerous showers today as low pressure\nwithin the mid-upper levels of the atmosphere feeds supply of\nmoisture into the area from the south and east. The wave of low\npressure is easily identifiable in the satellite water vapor \nimagery and the concerning factor is the low is nearly \nstationary which means steering winds will remain stagnant with \nlittle or no geographic change in the position of the surface \nfront today. As such, expect persistent formation of showers \nwithin the vicinity of the front with heaviest of the rain today\nfalling over southside VA, just north of the NC border. Model \nsignals suggest 0.50 to 1.50 inches of rain will be common from \nSouth Boston west through Franklin and Henry Counties today in \naddition to areas farther west along the Hwy 58 corridor. \nIsolated amounts of up to 3 inches may occur if thunderstorms \ndevelop. \n\nThe overall flash flood risk has decreased compared to previous\ndays, PWATs coming down from the near 2\" observed over the\nweekend...hovering closer to 1.50\" now. However, this is still \nrespectable, close to the 90th percentile, and with steering \nflow aloft coming to a halt, we may need to issue a flood watch \nfor parts of the forecast area if thunderstorm activity becomes \nmore widespread. Forecast mean winds for the piedmont are under\n5 kts from the south this afternoon, and out of the east at \nless than 10 kts for the foothills and mountains. Storms that \ndevelop today will come from a direction folks may not be use to\nseeing, the upper low inducing more of an east to west tendency\nto the cell movement today.\n\nAway from the convergent zone near the surface front, not \nexpecting a lot of shower coverage. However, heating of the day\ninstability driven showers are still possible but they should \nnot be as organized as what is expected along the actual surface\nfront. The Storm Prediction Center has highlighted a small\nmarginal risk area for strong to severe storms over western\nNorth Carolina...an area closer to the center of the upper low.\nAn axis of moderate instability is forecast to develop over the\nwestern Carolinas by afternoon. Warming surface temperatures in\nthe higher terrain will result in scattered convective \ninitiation across the southern Appalachians. As instability \npeaks and low-level lapse rates become steep in the afternoon, \nan isolated wind-damage threat is expected to develop in the \nwestern Carolinas.\n\nBy tonight, loss of daytime heating should mitigate any severe\nthreat, but a localized excessive rain hazard will linger. The \nsurface front over southern VA should begin to lift north as the\nupper low begins to open up and shear out. This should\nreconfigure the axis of low level convergence with areas closer\nto the I-64 corridor targeted for the higher qpf overnight.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Unsettled weather will persist through the rest\nof this week with daily chances of showers and thunderstorms.\n\nThe upper low over the Mid-Atlantic will shear out and weaken\nWednesday. Meanwhile a large upper level ridge builds over \nBermuda and another upper level trough approaches the Great \nLakes. Wind flow between these two features will maintain a\npersistent flow of moisture into the forecast area, enough to\nfuel daily showers and thunderstorms, most of which should\ncoincide with the peak heating of the day favoring the afternoon\nand evening hours. We are not expecting anything severe, but a\nmarginal risk for flash flooding will continue to be a hazard\ndue to the potential for high rain rates which may lead to\nrunoff issues.",
